Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Memory-context chunks as Sublet lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE)
Upstream: postgresql 17.0
Upstream-Archive: https://ftp.postgresql.org/pub/source/v17.0/postgresql-17.0.tar.bz2
Upstream-SHA256: 7e276131c0fdd6b62588dbad9b3bb24b8c3498d5009328dba59af16e819109de
Build-Variants: Sublet replay; the 17.5 server with PGSU_NESTED=sublet
Depends-On: postgresql-17.0-0001-allocset-capstone-size-classes.patch
  postgresql-17.0-0002-memorychunk-capstone-alignment.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Every chunk a memory context hands out is a child lifetime of its block,
bounded to its header and the request; pfree and repalloc revoke it, so
every copy of the caller's pointer dies with it. The 17.0 and 17.5
manager sources are identical, so the same patch serves the replay and
the server.

# Two instructions do the work. CDERIVE makes a child capability of a
# non-linear parent, bounded to a sub-range and carrying MANAGE over its own
# children; CREVOKE(parent, child) ends a direct child and everything below
# it, and faults when the child is not a live direct child.
#
# Every block carries two lifetimes in its header: life, derived from the
# malloc'd object, and the generation below it. Where a manager returns a
# chunk, MemoryChunkIssue derives the caller's pointer from the generation,
# covering the chunk header and the request, and stores the generation in
# the header. So pfree needs nothing but the pointer: it reads the parent
# from the header, revokes the chunk, and hands the context method a pointer
# at the same address derived from the parent. repalloc does the same and
# issues the result again when the manager kept the chunk in place.
#
# Freeing a block revokes the system allocator's lifetime of it, and every
# chunk lies below. A reset that keeps a block (AllocSet, Generation, Bump
# keepers) revokes its generation and derives a new one. Bump chunks have no
# header and no pfree; bump derives them itself, and gives a zero-byte
# request one MAXALIGN unit, because CDERIVE takes no empty child.
#
# The caller's pointer can read the header in front of its chunk, as it can
# without the patch, and so can load the generation: block-wide bounds with
# MANAGE. A stale pointer cannot, since it is revoked; a live one handed to
# code that loads the header deliberately gets the block. Overwriting the
# stored parent clears its tag and the next pfree faults. The chunk header
# grows from 16 to 32 bytes, and every block header by two capabilities.
